# Git & GitHub Workflow Security

## Repository Access Control

- **Always** protect `main`/`master` with branch protection rules
- Require PR reviews (at least 1 reviewer), dismiss stale reviews on new commits
- Require status checks to pass before merging
- Enable `Require review from Code Owners` with CODEOWNERS file
- **Never** allow admins to bypass branch protection — enable `Do not allow bypassing`
- **Never** allow force pushes to protected branches
- Disable forking on private repositories unless explicitly needed
- Enable automatic deletion of merged head branches

## Authentication & Credentials

- **Never** use `git config credential.helper store` — stores plaintext in `~/.git-credentials`
- Use secure credential helpers: `osxkeychain` (macOS), `libsecret` (Linux), `manager` (Windows)
- Use Ed25519 SSH keys with passphrase: `ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -a 100`
- Set PAT expiration (max 90 days). Migrate classic PATs to fine-grained (repo-scoped)
- **Never** embed tokens in clone URLs: `git clone https://user:token@github.com/...`
- **Never** commit `.git-credentials` — add to `.gitignore`
- **Never** set `http.sslVerify false` — fix the certificate issue instead

## Branch & Tag Security

- **Never** `git push --force` to shared branches — use `--force-with-lease` if force push is needed
- **Never** `git branch -D` unless certain the branch has no unique commits — use `-d` (safe delete)
- **Never** move published tags — protect tags in branch protection settings
- Enable signed commits (`commit.gpgsign = true`) and signed tags (`tag.gpgsign = true`)
- Verify signatures: `git log --show-signature` and `git tag -v <tag>`

## Merge, Rebase & Conflict Resolution

- **Never** rebase commits already pushed to shared branches — use merge instead
- **Never** `git reset --hard` without first stashing or branching: `git stash` or `git branch backup`
- **Never** merge with failing CI checks — enable required status checks
- After merge conflict resolution, **always** run the full test suite
- Review squash merge diffs as carefully as regular merges — squashing can obscure changes
- Audit `git stash` entries for secrets: `git stash show -p` before sharing machines
- For conflict resolution in security-critical files (auth, validation, .gitignore, lock files), see the critical-workflows rules

## Push & Pull Security

- Always clone via SSH or HTTPS — **never** `git clone http://` (unencrypted)
- **Never** push to main directly — use feature branches and PRs
- Audit submodule sources in `.gitmodules` — **never** reference untrusted repositories
- Use full clone (not `--depth 1`) for security scanning — shallow clones miss historical secrets
- Verify remote URLs: `git remote -v` — watch for typosquatted org names

## Commit History Security

- Require signed commits on protected branches to prevent author spoofing
- **Never** interpolate commit messages in shell commands (same injection risk as PR title)
- Secrets "removed" in later commits are still in history — rotate and purge with `git filter-repo`
- Review squash merge diffs carefully — squashing can hide security-weakening changes

## GitHub Organization Hardening

- Require 2FA for all organization members
- Set base repository permissions to `No permission` or `Read` — grant write per-team
- Have 2-5 organization owners (not 1, not >5)
- Audit outside collaborators quarterly — remove inactive ones
- Monitor audit log for: role changes, visibility changes, branch protection changes
- Enable SAML SSO for enterprise organizations

## Deploy Keys & Webhooks

- Deploy keys should be **read-only** unless writes are strictly necessary
- **Never** share the same deploy key across multiple repositories
- **Always** set webhook secrets and verify `X-Hub-Signature-256` on every delivery
- **Never** use `http://` for webhook payload URLs — always HTTPS
- Review GitHub App permissions — grant minimum required
- Installation tokens expire after 1 hour — don't cache beyond expiry

## Git Configuration Security

- Set secure credential helper (not `store`)
- Create global `.gitignore` with secret patterns: `*.pem`, `*.key`, `.env`, `credentials.*`
- Enable commit signing globally: `git config --global commit.gpgsign true`
- Install pre-commit hooks (`detect-secrets`, `gitleaks`, `no-commit-to-branch`)
- Audit git aliases for dangerous commands (`push --force`, `reset --hard`)
- Use `core.autocrlf = input` to standardize line endings and prevent diff noise hiding real changes
